	(text "In an ESC design like AM32, those 7× 47 µF capacitors from VBAT to GND are the main bulk-decoupling network for the high-current DC bus.\nHere's why so many-and why it's necessary:\n\n1. Absorbing Current Spikes from the MOSFET Bridge\n\nEach time a MOSFET phase leg switches, the motor windings create very fast di/dt pulses.\n\nThe battery leads have inductance; without local capacitance the voltage would ring or overshoot, potentially destroying the MOSFETs and MCU.\n\nMultiple low-ESR electrolytics or polymer caps right at the FETs supply these instantaneous currents.\n\n2. Reducing ESR and ESL\n\nA single large capacitor has higher Equivalent Series Resistance (ESR) and Equivalent Series Inductance (ESL).\n\nParalleling several caps divides ESR/ESL:\n\nESR_total ≈ ESR_single / N\n\nESL_total ≈ ESL_single / N\n\nLower ESR means lower ripple voltage and less heating.\n\n3. Handling Ripple Current & Heat\n\nEach cap has a ripple-current rating.\n\nSpreading the ripple across 7 parts keeps each one within spec and prolongs life.\n\n4. Distributing Capacitance Physically\n\nPlacing several smaller capacitors along the DC bus keeps the impedance low across the whole board, not just at one point.\n\nTypical Rule of Thumb\n\nHigh-power BLDC ESCs often target ≈300-400 µF per 100 A of phase current with low-ESR capacitors placed as close as possible to the MOSFET drain supply.\nSeven × 47 µF ≈ 330 µF is in line with a 30-60 A class ESC.\n\nIf You Reduce Them\n\nFewer or lower-value caps → higher voltage ripple → more stress on MOSFETs and driver ICs → possible shoot-through or gate-driver failure.\n\nYou might get away with fewer if the current is much lower than the design spec, but it's risky."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 335.28 469.9 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "c68bfc94-856f-4d7a-b7fb-58545a300ba8")
	)
